  "plot_summary": "The action picks up right where last issue left off, with the Thing hurling himself into combat with the Hulk, who thought he was done with him.&nbsp; The on-looking army guys admire the Thing's courage and point out that \"good thing they're battling in a condemned neighborhood!&nbsp; They're saving the wreckers a lot of work!\"&nbsp; (Holy cow, <i>another</i> one!)\n\nMeanwhile, we check in on Reed, who desperately wants to help Ben but can't due to the raging fever inside him, and Sue, who won't leave his side.&nbsp; The Human Torch wakes up in the hospital and manages to fly out to help Ben, but all he does is distract and anger him!&nbsp; The Hulk claims he's \"been betrayed by everyone  I've ever known - but no more!&nbsp; From now on, the <i>Hulk </i>strikes first!\"&nbsp; Soon, the Torch is too weak to continue, and Ben turns back to the battle - \"this business of bein' the idol of millions gets tougher all the time!\"\n\nBut the Hulk seems to have suddenly vanished.&nbsp; We find him below, actually hijacking a subway and driving it - he even gives the driver time to evacuate the passengers! (<i>whoa - different Hulk than the one I grew up reading</i>)&nbsp; He's still on a mission to find the Avengers!&nbsp; Once he reaches the \"expensive section of New York's upper east side,\" he emerges from a manhole and approaches \"Anthony Stark's mansion.\"\n\nHe walks right in on the Avengers, this time with Captain America.&nbsp; Rick Jones is also present and wants to reason with him.&nbsp; But the Hulk is looking <i>specifically</i> for him, who he feels left him for the Avengers!&nbsp; But the close quarters allow Hulk to grab Rick and crash out through a wall.&nbsp; Only the Wasp has the vantage point to see where he left and follow!\n\nThankfully, in a city hospital across town, the antidote worked on Reed Richards, and he immediately suits up, against his doctor's orders to recuperate.&nbsp; Johnny has now had time to rest a bit as well, so the FF are all back together at last!&nbsp; They overhear the army guys saying that word has arrived that the Avengers are pursuing the Hulk!&nbsp; So the FF jump into the Fantasti-Car (oh good, I had wondered whatever happened to it in the last issue - I guess it was indeed still hovering above all the action - <i>yeah right</i>).\n\nThey approach the Hulk, whose ear the Wasp is buzzing around in, trying to give Rick Jones a chance to escape, but the FF have arrived at the exact same time as the Avengers!&nbsp; They begin to get in each other's way, even to the point where the Thing accidentally gets in the way of Thor's hammer (the one thing Hulk fears, according to the text).&nbsp; When Ben sizes up Thor, he can't resist asking \"Say, Curly - is that getup of yours for <i>real</i>?\" They finally agree that working together, they might be able to stop the Hulk, to which Ben says to Thor \"Say, for a guy with long hair, you're A-okay!&nbsp; You've even got muscles in your <i>voice</i>!\"\n\nThe Hulk has been spotted by the police atop a new construction site, where Torch finds him confronting Rick Jones, angry about his apparent team-up with Captain America.&nbsp; Torch attacks, but Iron Man hurls him aside with his repulsors, using too much power, which blows up Torch's flame, requiring a rescue by Reed.&nbsp; But the Hulk pins Iron Man in some steel girders, requiring a rescue by Thor (who actually says \"Return to me, my enchanted mallet!\")\n\nBen then has had time to climb the girders and declares to the Hulk that <i>this </i>time, he's \"takin' off the kid gloves!\"&nbsp; But the force of their punches knocks the Thing into a vat of wet cement.&nbsp; Captain America and then Giant-Man have a go at the Hulk before the Wasp brings a cavalry of ants, which make the Hulk itch all over, giving Rick Jones time to hurl a \"gamma-ray treated capsule which Banner gave me months ago\" into his mouth!&nbsp; The Hulk heads toward the Hudson River to try and get the ants off of him.&nbsp; He disappears into its depths and seemingly vanishes, long enough for the FF and the Avengers to think the battle is over, but the reader is shown the face of Dr. Banner, who surfaces \"a few minutes later.\"\n\nIn the aftermath, Rick points out that he knew he was never really in any danger, and that his \"association\" with Captain America was really more because he reminded Cap of Bucky.&nbsp; Both teams acknowledge that it was good to have met the other.&nbsp; The FF walk off.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A mostly by-the-numbers plot, with the team-up of the Avengers and the FF being the major selling point of the book, but without any genuine payoff of lasting consequence.&nbsp; I thought the last issue was better, even with its questionable \"Reed gets sick\" and \"Torch gets hurt\" aspects to prevent them from getting involved, leaving us with the Thing/Hulk battle which really was the showcase of that issue.&nbsp; The fact of the matter is that the whole sequence probably lasted no more than an hour or two, but during that time, Reed is miraculously cured, and the Torch also finds the strength to fly back into battle.\n\nThe two teams getting in each other's way was fine - expected even, as there were a lot of people going at the Hulk at the same time.&nbsp; But as the number of pages left began to dwindle, Kirby was forced to wind things down with the introduction of the ants to drive him away.&nbsp; The fact that no one even <i>tried</i> to locate him after that is suspect and affected this issue's final rating (not to mention that miraculous \"gamma pill\" that Rick throws into the Hulk's mouth - <i>really</i>?).&nbsp; I'm not sure where the Hulk will show up next, but perhaps at least Rick had time enough to get it into his head that he didn't betray him - we can only hope!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis book's \"cool\" and \"wow\" factors weren't enough (in my mind) to elevate it even to an average grade.&nbsp; Seeing the teams together was interesting, sure, but not enough for me.&nbsp; There simply were too many characters running around to make this compelling enough to be a really memorable tale.\n\nRetro-Reviewer's Note:&nbsp; I acknowledge that from my perspective of one who began his comics journey in the middle of the Bronze Age (1977) that team-ups between Marvel characters were commonplace, and this issue doesn't impress as much as it might have to the readers plunking down their 12 cents in 1964.&nbsp; This issue may well have been a massive deal then, and I'm just not really appreciating it.&nbsp; This could well be the case, and even the \"Next Issue\" blurb about the upcoming team-up with Dr. Strange would have excited the readers of the day no end.&nbsp; I'll try and keep this in mind as I continue this project.\n\nSide Notes:\nI know that the final battle occurred on a new construction site, but otherwise, the continued availability of \"deserted\" and \"abandoned\" and even \"condemned\" structures (and in this issue, a whole <i>neighborhood</i>) in the middle of NYC continues to shock me.\n\nThe response to the letter by Reed J. Keonte was interesting in that it posed a contest where the readers write in to say what they think \"The Marvel Age of Comics\" actually means and states \"As usual, we'll award no prizes!\"&nbsp; As someone who's actually <i>seen</i> an official No-Prize that was owned by a college friend of mine (circa 1980), this makes me wonder when the practice started.&nbsp; I'll definitely keep my eyes peeled for the first official no-prize award!&nbsp; (In the meantime, the results of this was provided in the letters page of FF #31.)\n\nI think Bruce Solof's letter nicely summarizes what Marvel Comics meant to people of the day - here it is, in its entirety:\n<i>Dear Stan and Jack, </i>\n<i>I am glad to see all these Marvel Comics.&nbsp; I'm starting to outgrow other companies' super-heroes but I don't want to give up comics.&nbsp; Now I don't have to because MARVEL is more adult.</i>\n\n(Wow, that's a genuine testimonial if I've ever read one!)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}